### 3. Best Practices for Firebase Security

#### A. Firestore Security Rules
Your rules must allow the initial document creation from the Flutter client (Turn 10) while restricting sensitive updates (like `status`) to the backend. 

```javascript
service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {
    match /users/{userId} {
      // Allow users to read/create their own document
      allow read, create: if request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == userId;

      // Allow updates only if the user is the owner
      // and prevent them from manually setting their own 'status' to active
      allow update: if request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == userId 
                   && !request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys().hasAny(['status']);
    }
  }
}
```

#### B. Storage Rules
Align the rules with the path established in Turn 10 (`profiles/$uid.jpg`).
```javascript
match /profiles/{userId}.jpg {
  allow write: if request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == userId;
  allow read: if request.auth != null;
}
```

